#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069920 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ and the vertical line $x=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/65/651d5d0d9ccafdeb94608e74086136b121c394670afbea669c3e99606f9756aa.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/7a/7a1e4b8d0a27283cad7a0b0e0f2b23f1a45d0e560582a61f84c0f90a4f61e039.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $y=f(x),$ the inner curve $y=g(x),$ and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\Big( \\big[ \\underbrace{f(x)}_\\text{outer} \\big]^2 - \\big[ \\underbrace{g(x)}_\\text{inner} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method of computing the volume is called the **washer method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below). \n\n![](images/29/2920f308f15ef81eaff0ba431d0f4eec3ccd9826fbde8f126242b0493f1f8e32.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ so the area of each washer cross-section is \n\n$$\n\\pi [f(x)]^2 - \\pi [g(x)]^2,\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our case, the outer curve is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and the inner curve is $g(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((\\frac{4}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ cubic units." :tutorial/id #uuid "26f7c4eb-b5c8-505b-9621-32b5c0241376" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1058 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069921 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region bounded by the curve ![](images/da/da9e7c622c9bb4816126baaf7030000b0df743977b620a20454f6f41974d4a66.svg) the curve ![](images/34/344c03d67f3618b1dc0560c072fc86443bf8eb991de0eed6ed1196fe20586053.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/51/5105b7bf189fe285a6ab74d0f01cdea25d5acb40cac53551b0ac58def8d4180f.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3d071079a6f434bf7646a0fedef9d8670cf4d4d3809b6e2b42c5f2ba6833cd.png)\n\nWe rotate this region ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/67/67e20ea4be60086726fa1dfbede3d378f9b73e4ec57f8af722e8cebf307cd385.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the same washer method approach that we used earlier for the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. The only difference is that we need to swap ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) in our formula.\n\nSo, to find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve ![](images/fb/fb5fe1228a41ca0e72e76733ab39448868766741ac776c45aecea72e44f44f90.svg) the inner curve ![](images/5c/5c619f7c19342f727ea87a18b5934867e3e338a4fc0d8257d5943a585274f9b7.svg) and the horizontal lines ![](images/f8/f82a3483d5e6e5035663e40a89075138ad85d12d